# CloudTasks Design Patterns

## Documentation
Read docs/DESIGN_GUIDE.md for complete architecture and patterns.

## Docstrings
Follow the style guide in docs/DESIGN_GUIDE.md:
- Brief one-liners: """Action the noun"""
- With detail: """Action noun (detail)"""  
- With side effect: """Action noun and side effect"""
- Drop "for user" unless contrasting multiple users
- Skip docstrings for trivial getters/setters
- Help understand WHAT, not HOW

Examples:
- ✅ """Get active task session (4 tasks)"""
- ✅ """Complete task and refresh session"""
- ✅ """Ensure minimum tasks per category with locking"""
- ❌ """Get all goals for current user organized by category""" (too wordy)

## Architecture
Layered pattern: Routes → Services → Core → Database

Services (src/services/):
- Business logic and API contracts
- Request validation
- Error handling
- Response formatting

Core (src/core/):
- Domain logic and orchestration
- Master classes: ensure minimums, session management
- Generator classes: AI integration, creation logic

## File Organization
- One class per file
- Naming: {domain}_service.py, {domain}_master.py, {domain}_generator.py
- Single responsibility principle
- Group by responsibility, not by feature

## Code Style
- Type hints on function signatures
- Clear, descriptive variable names
- No global state
- Explicit dependency injection
- Keep functions focused

## Documentation Philosophy
Only create docs that matter in 6+ months:
- ✅ Permanent reference docs (README, DESIGN_GUIDE, API_CONTRACTS)
- ❌ No "COMPLETE", "PROGRESS", "SUMMARY" docs
- Use /plans/ for active work (delete when done)
- Keep /docs/ minimal and valuable
- Prefer inline docstrings over separate documentation

Rule: If a future developer won't need it to understand the system, don't create a separate doc for it.

